WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:10.100 position:63%
In the 1340s, the greatest pandemic in history&nbsp;
ravaged Eurasia: the plague. But how did the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:16.400 position:63%
disease even reach Europe? The gruesome story,&nbsp;
recounted time and again in encyclopedias,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.900 position:63%
documentaries, and YouTube videos , goes&nbsp;
like this: During the siege of Caffa in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:27.700 position:63%
Crimea from 1345 to 1347, the Mongols hurled&nbsp;
the bodies of their plague victims over the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:34.000 position:63%
walls into the city with catapults, “in the&nbsp;
hope that the intolerable stench would kill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:41.000 position:63%
everyone inside.” As the defenders fled,&nbsp;
they spread the disease throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:47.200 position:63%
Mediterranean region. The Black Death was thus&nbsp;
allegedly the result of biological warfare.&nbsp;

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:52.800 position:63%
This narrative of Caffa as the gateway through&nbsp;
which the plague entered Europe has become an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:59.500 position:63%
integral part of the history of epidemics and&nbsp;
biological warfare—no doubt in part because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:05.000 position:63%
it is so spectacular. The problem is that the&nbsp;
more closely historians have examined the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:12.600 position:63%
the more inconsistencies have emerged.&nbsp;
But is it actually false? And if so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:19.200 position:63%
how did the plague really reach Europe? That’s&nbsp;
exactly what we’ll be looking at in this video.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.200 --> 00:01:21.600 position:63%
Let’s start with the siege of Caffa.
Part 1: The Siege of Caffa&nbsp;

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:37.900 position:63%
In the 13th and early 14th centuries, the Mongol&nbsp;
Empire stretched across vast swaths of Eurasia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:45.700 position:63%
Its relative internal stability—often referred to&nbsp;
as the Pax Mongolica—fostered long-distance trade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.700 --> 00:01:52.100 position:63%
and gave the emerging Italian maritime republics&nbsp;
access to the markets of the Near East and Asia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:57.800 position:63%
In 1266, with the consent of the Khan of the&nbsp;
Golden Horde, Genoa founded the trading post&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:05.093 position:63%
of Caffa, which quickly became a central hub for&nbsp;
Black Sea trade—despite fluctuating relations with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.093 --> 00:02:12.293 position:63%
the Mongols. The network that emerged as a result&nbsp;
would have far-reaching consequences. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.293 --> 00:02:18.993 position:63%
when Jani Beg came to power in 1342, tensions&nbsp;
escalated because the new khan regarded the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.993 --> 00:02:24.293 position:63%
Italian merchants as guests in his territory&nbsp;
and sought to tighten his control over them.&nbsp;

00:02:25.593 --> 00:02:32.593 position:63%
A year later, an incident shattered the fragile&nbsp;
peace. Venice had a rival trading post in Tana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.293 --> 00:02:40.693 position:63%
now known as Azov. A violent clash broke&nbsp;
out there in September 1343. Haji ‘Umar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.693 --> 00:02:45.493 position:63%
a local administrator and tax collector,&nbsp;
became embroiled in an escalating dispute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.493 --> 00:02:52.393 position:63%
with the merchant Andreolo Civrano. The Venetian&nbsp;
took bloody revenge by ambushing Haji ‘Umar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.393 --> 00:02:58.093 position:63%
and killing his entire family.
The khan would not let that go unanswered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.293 --> 00:03:05.793 position:63%
