00:04:55.094 --> 00:05:01.594 position:63%
Jani Beg subsequently demanded the murderer’s&nbsp;
extradition, but the Venetians refused. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.594 --> 00:05:05.794 position:63%
response, the khan launched a campaign&nbsp;
to expel the Italians from the Black&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.794 --> 00:05:12.294 position:63%
Sea region. He arrested the Europeans&nbsp;
in his capital, Sarai, occupied Tana,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.294 --> 00:05:18.294 position:63%
and confiscated the merchants’ property. The&nbsp;
Venetians fled from Tana to the Black Sea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.294 --> 00:05:24.194 position:63%
and sought refuge in the well-fortified&nbsp;
city of Caffa. Jani Beg followed them.&nbsp;

00:05:26.494 --> 00:05:31.694 position:63%
The Golden Horde had already laid siege to the&nbsp;
city when envoys from the Venetian Senate came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.694 --> 00:05:38.194 position:63%
before Jani Beg. They informed him that Venice&nbsp;
strongly condemned Civrano’s actions and had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.194 --> 00:05:45.394 position:63%
exiled him along with his accomplices. But that&nbsp;
was not enough for the khan: by refusing to hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.394 --> 00:05:52.094 position:63%
Civrano over to Mongol authorities, the Venetians&nbsp;
were challenging his authority, and he would not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.094 --> 00:05:59.094 position:63%
tolerate that. He continued the siege.
In February 1344, a relief fleet from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.094 --> 00:06:04.494 position:63%
Italy finally appeared in the Black Sea. The&nbsp;
threat from the sea distracted the Mongols&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.494 --> 00:06:09.595 position:63%
long enough for the defenders to launch a&nbsp;
sortie. They stormed out of the fortress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.596 --> 00:06:15.496 position:63%
overran the siege lines, and burned the khan’s&nbsp;
siege engines. This setback and the arrival&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.496 --> 00:06:21.996 position:63%
of reinforcements dashed Jani Beg’s hopes. He&nbsp;
destroyed the rest of his catapults and withdrew.&nbsp;

00:06:23.296 --> 00:06:30.396 position:63%
This success encouraged Venice and Genoa to put&nbsp;
pressure on the khan in response. They set aside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.396 --> 00:06:37.296 position:63%
their long-standing rivalry, formed a temporary&nbsp;
alliance, and imposed a so-called devetum—a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.296 --> 00:06:44.296 position:63%
trade embargo against the Golden Horde. As we&nbsp;
will see in the second part, this would play a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.296 --> 00:06:52.796 position:63%
decisive role in the spread of the plague. Soon,&nbsp;
unsold goods were piling up in Solkhat and Sarai,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.796 --> 00:06:59.096 position:63%
and merchants were clamoring ever more loudly&nbsp;
for peace. But the khan would not yield. On&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.096 --> 00:07:05.296 position:63%
the contrary, he imposed a complete ban on grain&nbsp;
exports, which caused prices in the Mediterranean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.296 --> 00:07:11.896 position:63%
to skyrocket, since much of the region was&nbsp;
dependent on imports from the East. In some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.896 --> 00:07:18.996 position:63%
cities in the eastern Mediterranean, famine even&nbsp;
broke out. The situation continued to escalate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.996 --> 00:07:25.996 position:63%
until Jani Beg decided to march on Caffa once&nbsp;
more. Soon, the Mongol riders gathered in Solkhat.&nbsp;

00:07:26.896 --> 00:07:32.596 position:63%
In the second half of the year, Jani Beg ’s&nbsp;
army appeared once more before Caffa. Again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.596 --> 00:07:39.096 position:63%
the Mongols laid siege to the city—and again,&nbsp;
their blockade proved unsuccessful. The city’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.096 --> 00:07:44.296 position:63%
inhabitants had made thorough preparations and&nbsp;
stockpiled large supplies of military equipment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.296 --> 00:07:50.596 position:63%
and food. In addition, Caffa received supplies&nbsp;
through its harbor. Jani Beg also attempted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.596 --> 00:07:56.696 position:63%
blockade the city from the sea, but his fleet of&nbsp;
30 ships was no match for the Genoese navy, one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.696 --> 00:08:03.596 position:63%
the most advanced of its time. The Genoese sank&nbsp;
one Mongol ship after another; the route to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.596 --> 00:08:11.597 position:63%
harbor remained open, and the siege dragged on.
Two years later, the Golden Horde still had not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.597 --> 00:08:17.497 position:63%
conquered the city. Their attacks had repeatedly&nbsp;
crashed against the city walls without success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.197 --> 00:08:24.497 position:63%
and the rigors of the siege were taking their&nbsp;
toll. Worse still, in the fall an invisible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.497 --> 00:08:32.297 position:63%
enemy had struck them from the east: a strange and&nbsp;
deadly disease ravaged their ranks: “But behold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.297 --> 00:08:37.897 position:63%
the whole army was afflicted by a disease that&nbsp;
swept through the Tartars and killed thousands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.897 --> 00:08:44.797 position:63%
upon thousands every day. It was as though arrows&nbsp;
were raining down from heaven to strike and crush&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.797 --> 00:08:51.897 position:63%
the Tartars’ arrogance.” Soon the dead were piling&nbsp;
up at the edge of the camp, and Jani Beg had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.897 --> 00:08:58.197 position:63%
admit that he could not take the city under these&nbsp;
circumstances. And it was precisely here that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.197 --> 00:09:04.397 position:63%
controversial episode involving the bodies of&nbsp;
plague victims and catapults allegedly took&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.397 --> 00:09:11.597 position:63%
place. Jani Beg allegedly resorted to a final,&nbsp;
vicious measure to inflict the greatest possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.597 --> 00:09:16.697 position:63%
damage on the unyielding defenders: he ordered&nbsp;
the corpses of plague victims to be brought to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.697 --> 00:09:23.097 position:63%
catapults and hurled into the city: “What seemed&nbsp;
like mountains of dead were thrown into the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.097 --> 00:09:29.297 position:63%
and the Christians could not hide or flee or&nbsp;
escape from them.” According to the sources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.297 --> 00:09:34.697 position:63%
the plan proved terrifyingly effective. The&nbsp;
disease spread rapidly throughout Caffa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.697 --> 00:09:39.297 position:63%
even though the inhabitants tried to protect&nbsp;
themselves by throwing the corpses into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.297 --> 00:09:46.197 position:63%
harbor. “And soon the rotting corpses tainted&nbsp;
the air and poisoned the water supply, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.197 --> 00:09:52.097 position:63%
stench was so overwhelming that hardly one in&nbsp;
several thousand was in a position to flee the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.097 --> 00:09:59.897 position:63%
remains of the Tartar army.” Although Jani Beg was&nbsp;
forced to withdraw a second time in early 1347,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.897 --> 00:10:06.097 position:63%
his attack had supposedly brought the Black Death&nbsp;
to Caffa. The defenders were forced to abandon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.097 --> 00:10:12.098 position:63%
the city and flee. In doing so, they carried&nbsp;
the deadly epidemic into the Mediterranean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.098 --> 00:10:16.698 position:63%
and spread it through the major ports of&nbsp;
the Middle East, North Africa, and Europe:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.698 --> 00:10:22.698 position:63%
“It was as if they had brought evil spirits with&nbsp;
them: every city, every settlement, every place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.698 --> 00:10:29.898 position:63%
was poisoned by the contagious pestilence.”
If this traditional account is true, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.898 --> 00:10:35.898 position:63%
would represent one of the earliest instances of&nbsp;
biological warfare, with unintended but disastrous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.898 --> 00:10:42.498 position:63%
consequences. But although it seems quite&nbsp;
plausible at first glance—and it makes sense that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.498 --> 00:10:47.798 position:63%
the siege of Caffa might have been the starting&nbsp;
point for the pandemic—almost all the evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.798 --> 00:11:00.698 position:63%
today suggests that this story is false.
Part 2: Catapults That Hurled Corpses?&nbsp;

00:11:00.698 --> 00:11:05.498 position:63%
The account of the events during the second siege&nbsp;
of Caffa comes from one of the earliest accounts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.498 --> 00:11:10.998 position:63%
of the plague, the “Report on the Plague and&nbsp;
the Great Mortality in the Year of Our Lord&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.998 --> 00:11:19.698 position:63%
1348” by the Italian notary Gabriele de Mussi.&nbsp;
And here we already encounter a major problem:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.698 --> 00:11:25.298 position:63%
De Mussi was not actually in Caffa during the&nbsp;
siege, as had long been assumed. The dates on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.298 --> 00:11:30.398 position:63%
the documents he issued in his capacity&nbsp;
as a notary prove that he remained in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.398 --> 00:11:40.698 position:63%
hometown of Piacenza—over 1,000 km away—between&nbsp;
1344 and 1356. De Mussi was therefore not an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.698 --> 00:11:46.998 position:63%
eyewitness. He likely compiled his account from&nbsp;
the stories of merchants and travelers and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.998 --> 00:11:52.698 position:63%
incorporated it into his history of the plague.
That does not mean, however, that his account&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.698 --> 00:11:58.198 position:63%
of events is completely false. Large parts of&nbsp;
the narrative are plausible and can even be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.198 --> 00:12:04.198 position:63%
corroborated by other contemporary reports. For&nbsp;
example, the Mongols did indeed besiege Caffa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.198 --> 00:12:10.198 position:63%
and the plague did indeed strike the Golden Horde.&nbsp;
However, the claim that the Mongols used catapults&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.198 --> 00:12:14.699 position:63%
to hurl the bodies of plague victims into the&nbsp;
city, thereby facilitating the spread of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:21.000 position:63%
plague to Europe, is questionable. De Mussi is&nbsp;
the only one who reports this alleged act of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.000 --> 00:12:27.000 position:63%
biological warfare and establishes a direct link&nbsp;
to the spread of the plague. A letter sent by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:33.900 position:63%
inhabitants of Caffa to their patron, the Doge of&nbsp;
Genoa, shortly after the siege does state that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:39.800 position:63%
city “was besieged for a long time using every&nbsp;
method capable of instilling terror” , but there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.800 --> 00:12:44.400 position:63%
is no indication that they were referring&nbsp;
to the use of catapults to hurl corpses.&nbsp;

00:12:45.400 --> 00:12:50.800 position:63%
There are four fundamental problems with de&nbsp;
Mussis’s account. First, in the Middle Ages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.800 --> 00:12:56.900 position:63%
it was anything but common to use human&nbsp;
remains as siege engine projectiles. Only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.900 --> 00:13:02.900 position:63%
a handful of cases have been recorded in&nbsp;
which this was done, and aside from Caffa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:07.800 position:63%
there are just two instances in which entire&nbsp;
bodies were allegedly hurled from catapults.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:15.100 position:63%
Because there are no eyewitness accounts of any&nbsp;
of these three incidents, Jean Pascal Zanders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:22.600 position:63%
an expert on biological warfare, is convinced that&nbsp;
these are narrative embellishments. If biological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.600 --> 00:13:29.000 position:63%
warfare was waged during this period, it was more&nbsp;
likely carried out by contaminating wells than by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:36.400 position:63%
using plague-ridden corpses as biological weapons.
Second, there are major questions regarding the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:41.500 position:63%
transmission of the disease. A number of studies&nbsp;
have shown that the plague can be transmitted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.500 --> 00:13:46.600 position:63%
through direct contact with infected corpses ,&nbsp;
and the defenders of Caffa would certainly have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:52.300 position:63%
been particularly vulnerable because of the the&nbsp;
siege. However, this also applies to the Mongols,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:57.300 position:63%
since they would have come into contact with&nbsp;
the bodies in exactly the same way during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.300 --> 00:14:02.300 position:63%
such an attack. While the Mongols did not know&nbsp;
that the plague was transmitted by a bacterium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:08.800 position:63%
they were aware of the risks associated with&nbsp;
coming into contact with sick people. If they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:14.000 position:63%
understood that the plague victims could be used&nbsp;
as biological weapons, it must also have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.000 --> 00:14:18.701 position:63%
clear to them that they were putting themselves&nbsp;
at risk by handling and transporting the bodies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.301 --> 00:14:24.701 position:63%
Furthermore, according to historian Marie&nbsp;
Favereau, the Mongols had great respect for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.701 --> 00:14:30.101 position:63%
their dead and would not have desecrated them&nbsp;
in this way, even in such an extreme situation.&nbsp;

00:14:31.201 --> 00:14:36.901 position:63%
Third, there is a very practical problem:&nbsp;
the siege engines themselves. It is unclear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.901 --> 00:14:42.201 position:63%
whether the Golden Horde even used catapults&nbsp;
during the second siege of Caffa, and if so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.201 --> 00:14:47.401 position:63%
how they would have been able to hurl&nbsp;
corpses over a city wall with them. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.401 --> 00:14:52.301 position:63%
the only medieval siege engine capable of&nbsp;
hurling a heavy, bulky object such as a corpse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.301 --> 00:14:58.301 position:63%
over a city wall would have been a counterweight&nbsp;
trebuchet. However, these massive machines had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.301 --> 00:15:04.301 position:63%
be assembled on site, which in this case would&nbsp;
have been well within range of the defenders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.301 --> 00:15:09.601 position:63%
who had the advantage of being positioned high&nbsp;
above them on the wall. Reconstructions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.601 --> 00:15:15.401 position:63%
experiments have shown that even large trebuchets&nbsp;
could barely hurl heavy projectiles more than 300&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:15.401 --> 00:15:23.201 position:63%
meters. To hurl a bulky corpse in an arc over a&nbsp;
wall, the Mongol siege engines would therefore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.201 --> 00:15:27.501 position:63%
have had to be so close that the defenders&nbsp;
could have destroyed them without difficulty.&nbsp;

00:15:29.201 --> 00:15:34.301 position:63%
Fourth, de Mussi’s account does not line up&nbsp;
with the chronology of the plague’s spread.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.801 --> 00:15:40.201 position:63%
If the disease had been spread by a wave of&nbsp;
refugees fleeing Caffa, it would have had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.201 --> 00:15:46.901 position:63%
reach the Mediterranean ports by the end of&nbsp;
1346 at the latest. However, it did not reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.901 --> 00:15:55.901 position:63%
Constantinople until September 1347, Alexandria&nbsp;
in October, Genoa in November, and Venice not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.901 --> 00:16:05.101 position:63%
until February 1348. In fact, there was likely no&nbsp;
mass exodus at all, as described by de Mussi. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.101 --> 00:16:10.301 position:63%
the letter mentioned earlier, the inhabitants&nbsp;
of Caffa asked the Doge of Genoa for money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.301 --> 00:16:16.301 position:63%
so that they could repair their defenses, pay&nbsp;
their troops, and recruit more men. This suggests&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.301 --> 00:16:21.102 position:63%
that they remained in the city, rebuilding&nbsp;
and strengthening it rather than fleeing.&nbsp;

00:16:21.903 --> 00:16:26.703 position:63%
Taking all this into account, it is extremely&nbsp;
unlikely that a biological attack at the siege of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:26.703 --> 00:16:34.003 position:63%
Caffa triggered the plague epidemic of the 1340s.&nbsp;
Presumably, de Mussi’s account reflects less an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.003 --> 00:16:39.703 position:63%
understanding of how the disease actually spread&nbsp;
than a centuries-old notion that diseases flowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.703 --> 00:16:45.203 position:63%
from the barbaric East into the civilized West.&nbsp;
In this sense, the image of the Mongols hurling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.203 --> 00:16:51.603 position:63%
the bodies of plague victims reinforces the idea&nbsp;
of an evil, inhuman threat from the East. But how,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:51.603 --> 00:16:56.503 position:63%
then, did the Black Death reach Europe?
Even if the siege of Caffa was not the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.503 --> 00:17:02.302 position:63%
pivotal event it was long believed to be, there&nbsp;
is a general consensus that the plague reached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.302 --> 00:17:08.602 position:63%
the Mediterranean region via the Black Sea, since&nbsp;
it had already broken out in Tana, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.603 --> 00:17:16.503 position:63%
as early as 1346. However, the decisive event was&nbsp;
not the siege, but rather the end of the conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.503 --> 00:17:22.503 position:63%
between the Italian maritime republics and the&nbsp;
Golden Horde in early 1347, as historian Hannah&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.503 --> 00:17:29.402 position:63%
Barker explains. As we have seen, Caffa was not&nbsp;
an isolated crisis point, but rather part of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:29.402 --> 00:17:35.702 position:63%
far-reaching trade network. From the 1330s onward,&nbsp;
the city was the center of Genoese trade with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.703 --> 00:17:41.703 position:63%
East. It was a transit port connecting the Black&nbsp;
Sea to the Sea of Azov and the terminus of one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.703 --> 00:17:47.603 position:63%
of the northern Silk Road routes. From the Sea of&nbsp;
Azov, the Don River led into the heart of Russia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.103 --> 00:17:54.003 position:63%
and various overland trade routes extended&nbsp;
to Sarai and Asia. Along these routes, silk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:54.003 --> 00:18:00.103 position:63%
spices, and other luxury goods traveled from&nbsp;
the Far East to Europe, as well as timber,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.103 --> 00:18:07.302 position:63%
wine, fish, and—most importantly—grain.
By the end of 1346, this trade had practically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.302 --> 00:18:13.102 position:63%
come to a standstill due to the mutual embargoes.&nbsp;
However, when the siege of Caffa ended, the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:13.103 --> 00:18:19.703 position:63%
sides made peace, and the trade restrictions&nbsp;
were lifted, goods began to flow again. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.703 --> 00:18:24.904 position:63%
the cities in Crimea imported large quantities&nbsp;
of food to feed their starving populations and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.904 --> 00:18:31.404 position:63%
to help combat the famine in the Mediterranean&nbsp;
region. Along with this grain, however, came rats;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:31.404 --> 00:18:37.204 position:63%
with the rats came fleas; and with the fleas&nbsp;
came the plague bacterium Yersinia pestis.&nbsp;

00:18:38.104 --> 00:18:43.504 position:63%
The rodents continued to multiply in the grain&nbsp;
stores of the Crimean cities. Consequently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:43.504 --> 00:18:49.404 position:63%
when the first ships sailed across the Black Sea&nbsp;
again in the summer of 1347, they carried not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.404 --> 00:18:56.504 position:63%
the long-awaited grain but also large numbers of&nbsp;
infected rats. The grain trade became the route&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.504 --> 00:19:03.204 position:63%
through which the plague spread. It broke out in&nbsp;
Constantinople in September 1347—shortly after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.204 --> 00:19:09.604 position:63%
the arrival of that summer’s grain harvest. From&nbsp;
there, merchant ships carried the deadly cargo to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:09.604 --> 00:19:13.904 position:63%
the major port cities of the Mediterranean,&nbsp;
paving the way for the Black Death to reach&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.904 --> 00:19:18.874 position:63%
Europe. The plague epidemic of the 1340s was,&nbsp;
in all likelihood, the unintended consequence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.874 --> 00:19:24.204 position:63%
medieval long-distance trade, and not the result&nbsp;
of an early biological attack by the Golden Horde.

